~D. k = 30#6 times 30 is not 30.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Which value checks correctly for <sup>s</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub> = 2? {
~A. s = 5#5 divided by 7 is not 2.
~B. s = <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>#This divides by 7 again instead of checking.
~C. s = 9#9 divided by 7 is not 2.
=D. s = 14#Correct. 14 divided by 7 is 2.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves x + 9 = 16 and gets x = 25. What is the error? {
=A. The student added 9 instead of subtracting 9.#Correct. Since 9 is added to x, subtract 9 from both sides.
~B. The student divided by 9.#There is no division by 9 in this equation.
~C. The student checked correctly.#25 + 9 is not 16.
~D. The student should leave x = 16.#x is not isolated until +9 is undone.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves w - 3 = 14 and gets w = 11. What is the error? {
~A. The student added 14 to both sides.#The error is with the inverse of -3, not with 14.
=B. The student subtracted 3 instead of adding 3.#Correct. Since 3 is subtracted, add 3 to both sides.
~C. The student divided by 3.#The result 11 comes from subtraction, not division.
~D. There is no error.#11 - 3 is 8, not 14.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves 5x = 45 and gets x = 225. What is the error? {
~A. The student subtracted 5.#225 comes from multiplication, not subtraction.
~B. The student added 5.#225 comes from multiplication, not addition.
=C. The student multiplied by 5 instead of dividing by 5.#Correct. Multiplication by 5 is undone by division by 5.
~D. There is no error.#5 times 225 is not 45.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>A student solves <sup>x</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub> = 4 and gets x = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>. What is the error? {
~A. The student added 8.#One-half suggests dividing again, not adding.
~B. The student subtracted 8.#Subtraction does not undo division by 8.
~C. There is no error.#One-half divided by 8 is not 4.
=D. The student divided by 8 instead of multiplying by 8.#Correct. Division by 8 is undone by multiplication by 8.
}

::ALG1_U02_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U02_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>No new primary MLA standard; prerequisite support.<br><br>Question:<br>Why should you check a solution in the original equation? {
=A. To verify that the value makes both sides equal#Correct. A solution must make the original equation true.
~B. To change the equation into an expression#Checking does not remove the equals sign.
~C. To avoid inverse operations#Inverse operations are still needed to solve.
~D. To make the answer larger#Checking verifies accuracy; it does not change the answer.
}

